From: vraxa <200693817+vraxa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:19:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Replay Plus 1.2: mouse and keyboard in the panel, skater
 autofocus, keep keys, video folder

- the panel takes the mouse (F10, or moving the mouse while it's open): hover, click, drag sliders, wheel,
  held arrows, clickable prompts; more keys (WASD, Space, Tab, Home / End, + / -, Shift for x10)
- a new EXPERIMENTAL tab for what reads the game's own replay:
  - Skater autofocus: Auto follows where the replay has the skater, from the keyframe manager
  - Keep keys: the game's camera keyframes, focus keys and shake keys come back when the editor opens again on the
    same footage; off, each visit starts clean; keys of an earlier game run are cleared at start
- Videos go to (VIDEO): exports moved to a folder of your own once done; folder picker; replayplus videofolder
- times on the timeline through the replay's chunks (marks and key times on replays opened again)
- the replay editor only counts while a replay is up: the park editor and the hub's skater view no longer get the
  lens and the shake
- the editor's picture now matches exported videos: under the game's UI the pass gets the game's picture before
  its last pass, which shows it through the game's display mapping (a filmic curve on each channel, its white far
  above 1). Taken as 0..1, a camera curve's white showed at 181 of 255 and the bright half went flat and grey,
  while exports (finished from the game's 8-bit frame) were right. In SDR the curve, measured from the screen
  (lens::DisplayMapping), is now applied to read the picture and undone to write it back; in HDR the picture's
  white is measured from the screen. replayplus white tells what it found (and white <value> / auto to compare)
- the render scale no longer goes up and down in the editor as the video memory Windows gives the game moves with
  what else is on the screen (each change remade the game's targets, a flash): once lowered, it stays for that visit
- the camera light no longer shows the game's meshes' triangles: it lights what is in its beam by its distance alone
  (which way a surface faces could only come from the game's depth, its meshes' flat triangles; smoothed over the
  surface, thin things still showed them), and its edge is soft as the picture's (the depth is not antialiased: a
  tent over each pixel's neighbours). replayplus lightfacing compares (none, smooth, triangles)
- the camera light keeps textures' contrast and does not flare other lights: it brightens the picture, and only what
  is very bright already (a lamp, a light dropped in with the object dropper, a glow) takes less of it; its lift for
  what is black is at most 0.05, so close up it does not grey the darks; and it rolls off into white as film does
  instead of clipping (a calf lit past white clipped in blocks where its skin's texture crossed the clip)
- R3 + dpad right turns the camera light on and off (it was a focus key here; dpad up / down still make those)
